\title{Does the Picture Matter?\\
Image Representation as an Unstudied Variable in\\
Citation-Grounded Multimodal LLM Classification of IoT Network Traffic}

\author[1]{Gangadhar Singh Shiva\thanks{Corresponding author: \texttt{gshiva@sandiego.edu}}}
\author[2]{Poonam Singh Umakanth\thanks{\texttt{poonam.singh5@gmail.com}}}
\affil[1]{University of San Diego}
\affil[2]{HPE Juniper Networking, Hewlett Packard Enterprise}

\date{August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle
\sloppy

\begin{abstract}
Prior work has shown that restricting a multimodal LLM's citation menu to a small, verified
reference corpus (retrieval-augmented, or ``RAG-grounded'' prompting) measurably reduces citation
hallucination when classifying IoT network traffic rendered as images, but that this effect can
interact with classification accuracy in model-dependent ways. That work held the \emph{image
representation} fixed -- a single four-panel composite chart (packet rate, TCP flag composition,
protocol mix, packet-size statistics) -- and varied only the prompting condition. This paper asks
the complementary question: with prompting condition held fixed at RAG-grounded, does the visual
\emph{encoding} of the identical underlying traffic data change how much the model hallucinates its
citations? We propose and implement a six-way image-representation comparison spanning a
deliberate annotation gradient -- from a fully labeled, legended four-panel chart, through a
single-channel line plot and a features$\times$time heatmap, to two representations with zero
textual annotation (a pairwise-similarity recurrence plot and a raw numeric-to-pixel grayscale
matrix) -- plus a sixth representation along a genuinely different axis: a 3-channel (red/green/blue)
numeric-to-pixel encoding that is 3-dimensional (height $\times$ width $\times$ channel) rather than
a 2D grid rendered through a colormap, mirroring multi-channel CNN-based intrusion-detection
encodings. All six hold constant the dataset (CIC-IoT-2023), the two models under test (Qwen-vl-plus
and GPT-4o-mini), the citation corpus, the grading rubric, and the prompting condition. To
our knowledge, no prior work has treated image-representation choice as an independent variable
for citation hallucination specifically, as distinct from the adjacent (but different) literatures
on time-series-to-image encoding for CNN-based intrusion detection and on vision-encoder-level
causes of multimodal LLM hallucination. This paper specifies the full experimental design, releases
working, resumable experiment code (six representation renderers, a fixed RAG-grounded prompting
harness for three model APIs, and citation-grading infrastructure reused unchanged from the prior
study), and reports results from a real, completed run against CIC-IoT-2023 ($n=1{,}020$ windows,
10 classes, 6 representations, 6,120 calls per model). Both models show a sharp, non-monotonic split:
three annotated representations (four\_panel, single\_line, heatmap) sustain 42--80\% classification
accuracy and 31--62\% correct-citation rates, while the three unannotated/dimensionality-varying
representations (recurrence\_plot, grayscale\_matrix, rgb\_stack) collapse to chance-level accuracy
($\approx$10\%, matching the 10-class base rate) for both models. Critically, this collapse is not a
retreat into honest uncertainty: both models continue to volunteer confident citations at low
unannotated-representation no-citation rates (0--16\%), and Qwen-vl-plus's collapse is total --
identical classification and citation output on literally every one of the 3,060 unannotated-image
calls, regardless of the true class. This is evidence for hallucination-through-fabrication (a
variant of H1) rather than hallucination-through-elaboration (H2): stripping annotation does not make
either model behave more conservatively, it makes both models guess with undiminished confidence.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A companion study~\cite{groundingcitation2026} established that when a multimodal LLM classifies
IoT network traffic rendered as an image and is asked to justify its classification with a
CVE/CAPEC citation, restricting the model's citation menu to a small, independently verified
reference corpus (RAG-grounded prompting) substantially improves citation correctness relative to
letting the model cite from its own training-time knowledge. That study varied \emph{prompting
condition} -- naive, text-grounded, or RAG-grounded -- while holding the image representation fixed
at a single four-panel composite chart designed for human readability: labeled axes, a legend on
every panel, and a title. That choice was reasonable for a first study, but it leaves a distinct
question unanswered: is a citation grounding technique's effectiveness a property of the
\emph{prompt}, or does it also depend on the \emph{image} -- specifically, on how much visual
annotation and semantic scaffolding that image carries?

This question matters practically. Real-world traffic-visualization pipelines will not all render
the same labeled four-panel chart a research paper does; some will produce minimal single-channel
plots, some will produce dense feature-matrix heatmaps for compact dashboards, and some -- notably,
representations borrowed from the CNN-based network-intrusion-detection literature, where
raw-byte and pairwise-similarity encodings are common precisely because they need no human-readable
annotation -- will produce images with no textual scaffolding at all. If citation hallucination
rates vary substantially across these encodings, then a grounding technique validated on one
representation cannot be assumed to transfer to a deployment that renders traffic differently, and
representation choice becomes a first-class design decision for any citation-grounded multimodal
LLM security pipeline, not an incidental implementation detail.

\paragraph{Two competing hypotheses.} We see no clear a priori winner between two plausible
mechanisms, which is itself part of the motivation for running the comparison rather than assuming
an answer:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{H1 -- annotation helps grounding.} A richly labeled image (axis labels, a legend
naming each traffic feature, a title) gives the model concrete, nameable visual anchors to tie a
citation to (``the TCP flag composition panel shows sustained SYN dominance, consistent with
CAPEC-482''). Stripping that annotation removes those anchors, so the model may fall back on
generic or fabricated justifications more often under low-annotation representations.
\item \textbf{H2 -- annotation invites elaboration, and elaboration invites hallucination.} A
richly annotated image may instead prompt a longer, more confident-sounding justification that
reaches further from the verified corpus text -- the same failure mode long-form RAG evaluations
call over-elaboration~\cite{factum2026}. A sparse, unlabeled image may instead push the model toward
shorter, more conservative responses (including more frequent, and appropriately honest,
N/A-citation answers), which would show up as \emph{lower} apparent citation-quality problems simply
because the model volunteers fewer citations to grade in the first place.
\end{itemize}

Section~\ref{sec:related} distinguishes this question from the two literatures it sits between.
Section~\ref{sec:methods} specifies the six representations, the fixed prompting condition, and the
full experimental protocol. Section~\ref{sec:results} reports results from a completed run against
the real CIC-IoT-2023 dataset. Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses what the observed pattern
implies for H1 and H2, and Section~\ref{sec:limitations} states this study's scope limits.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{A novel, isolated independent variable.} We hold prompting condition, dataset,
models, citation corpus, and grading rubric fixed and vary only image representation -- to our
knowledge the first study to treat traffic-image encoding as an independent variable for citation
hallucination specifically (see Section~\ref{sec:related} for how this differs from adjacent
image-encoding-for-IDS and MLLM-hallucination-causes literatures).
\item \textbf{A deliberate annotation gradient, plus a dimensionality axis.} Five representations
spanning full annotation to none, letting us distinguish an information-content effect (fewer
channels shown) from a pure annotation effect (same channels, no labels); a sixth, unannotated
representation (\texttt{rgb\_stack}) instead varies dimensionality itself -- a genuinely
3-dimensional (height $\times$ width $\times$ channel) encoding rather than a further step along the
2D annotation gradient.
\item \textbf{Reused, not re-derived, evaluation infrastructure.} The citation corpus and grading
rubric are the exact modules from the prior study~\cite{groundingcitation2026}, so any observed
representation effect cannot be an artifact of a differently calibrated grader.
\item \textbf{Working, resumable experiment code}, released alongside this paper, so the results
reported below can be reproduced or extended by re-running two scripts against the public
CIC-IoT-2023 dataset.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Time-series- and traffic-to-image encoding.} Rendering a time series as an image for
downstream CNN classification is an established technique. Wang and Oates~\cite{wang2015gaf}
introduced Gramian Angular Fields and Markov Transition Fields for encoding univariate time series
as images; recurrence plots -- the pairwise-similarity encoding we adopt as one of our six
representations -- are a related, older technique from nonlinear dynamics later adopted for the
same purpose. In the malware-analysis literature, Nataraj et al.~\cite{nataraj2011malware}
introduced the raw-byte grayscale encoding we adopt as another unlabeled representation, showing
that a file's raw bytes, reshaped into a 2D grayscale image with no semantic annotation, preserve
enough family-level visual texture for classification. Within network security specifically, this
same idea has been applied directly to traffic: time-series imaging has been used for wireless
link-layer anomaly detection~\cite{wirelessimaging2021}, and multiple recent CNN-based intrusion
detection systems encode flow features into 2D or multi-channel images, including a byte-level
flow-to-image encoding~\cite{byteflow2026} and CiNeT, a bijective, interpretable
encoding-decoding framework between network traffic features and RGB image representations
specifically~\cite{bijective2025} -- the direct precedent for our sixth representation,
\texttt{rgb\_stack}, which similarly stacks distinct feature groups as color channels rather than
rendering them as a single-channel 2D grid. All of this work, however, targets a \emph{CNN trained
end-to-end} on the resulting images for a classification label; none of it asks how a
general-purpose, zero-shot \emph{multimodal LLM} presented with these same encodings behaves, and
in particular none of it studies citation behavior, since a trained CNN classifier does not
generate citations at all. This paper borrows three of its six representations (recurrence plot,
raw grayscale matrix, RGB stack) directly from this literature's precedent, but asks a question
that literature has no mechanism to ask.

\paragraph{Multimodal LLM hallucination.} A substantial and growing literature studies why
multimodal LLMs hallucinate, surveyed comprehensively by Liu et al.~\cite{mllmhallucsurvey2024}.
Documented causes include vision-encoder limitations (resolution, salience bias), imbalanced
attention across visual tokens, spurious visual correlations, and a modality imbalance in which a
dominant language model overrides weaker visual evidence. This literature studies hallucination as
a property of \emph{how the model processes a given image} -- decoding strategy, attention
distribution, encoder architecture -- not as a property of \emph{which image, among several
alternative renderings of the same underlying data, the model was shown in the first place}. It
therefore does not directly address whether swapping a chart's visual encoding, with the model,
dataset, and prompt held fixed, changes citation-hallucination rates; that is the gap this paper
targets.

\paragraph{Citation hallucination and grounding.} The companion study~\cite{groundingcitation2026}
this paper extends showed that RAG-grounded prompting -- restricting a model's citation menu to a
small, independently verified corpus supplied in-context, in the spirit of
Lewis et al.'s retrieval-augmented generation~\cite{lewis2020retrieval} -- measurably improves
citation correctness for IoT traffic classification, replicated across two models at matched
$n=1{,}020$ scale, though with a model-dependent effect on raw classification accuracy traced to a
category-specific instance of the broader ``contextual distraction'' phenomenon documented in other
RAG evaluations~\cite{adaptivedistraction2025}. Mechanistic citation-hallucination detection has
also been studied directly in long-form RAG text generation~\cite{factum2026}. All of this prior
work, including our own companion study, holds image representation fixed. This paper is, to our
knowledge, the first to vary it while holding the grounding technique constant.

\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Dataset and traffic windows}

We reuse the real CIC-IoT-2023 dataset~\cite{cic-iot-2023}, the same public benchmark used in the
companion study, and the identical windowing procedure: each classified unit is a
\texttt{GROUP\_SIZE=30}-row window of the dataset's per-flow feature aggregation (each row already
summarizes $\sim$100 packets, so each window covers $\sim$3,000 packets), sampled without
replacement per class-file via a fixed random seed (\texttt{RNG = numpy.random.default\_rng(7)}),
identical to the companion study's sampling code. Reusing the exact windowing logic means any
representation effect we observe cannot be attributed to a different sampling procedure.

\subsection{Six image representations}
\label{sec:representations}

All six representations are computed from the identical underlying feature set for a given window
-- packet rate, five TCP-flag fractions (FIN/SYN/RST/PSH/ACK), six protocol-mix fractions
(TCP/UDP/HTTP/HTTPS/ICMP/DNS), and average/standard-deviation packet size (fourteen channels total)
-- so only the \emph{visual encoding} varies, not the information available to the model. The first
five are ordered deliberately along an annotation gradient, from full labeling to none; the sixth
(\texttt{rgb\_stack}) instead varies dimensionality, encoding three feature groups as the
red/green/blue channels of one color image rather than as a single-channel 2D grid:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{four\_panel.} The companion study's original composite: four stacked, labeled
subplots (rate line, flag-composition lines with a legend, a protocol-mix stacked area with a
legend, and avg/std packet-size lines with a legend), with a descriptive title. Richest annotation;
serves as the replication baseline against the companion study.
\item \textbf{single\_line.} A single labeled line plot of packet rate only -- the same annotation
richness as one panel of \texttt{four\_panel}, but with only one of the fourteen feature channels
shown. Included to separate an \emph{information-reduction} effect (fewer channels) from a pure
\emph{annotation-reduction} effect, which the remaining four representations isolate by keeping all
fourteen channels.
\item \textbf{heatmap.} A features$\times$time heatmap: each of the fourteen feature channels is a
labeled row, each window-step is a column, color (with a colorbar) encodes the per-feature
min-max-normalized value. Same information content as \texttt{four\_panel}, denser and differently
encoded, still fully annotated (row labels, axis label, colorbar).
\item \textbf{recurrence\_plot.} A square, entirely unlabeled grayscale image in which pixel
$(i,j)$ encodes the normalized Euclidean distance between window-step $i$'s and window-step $j$'s
full (per-feature-normalized) fourteen-dimensional feature vector. No axis labels, legend, or
colorbar. Encodes temporal self-similarity structure rather than raw feature values directly.
\item \textbf{grayscale\_matrix.} A raw numeric-to-pixel grayscale image: rows are window-steps,
columns are the same fourteen feature channels in a fixed order, pixel brightness is that feature's
per-channel min-max-normalized value. No axis labels, legend, or colorbar of any kind -- the direct
network-traffic analogue of the raw-byte malware-image encoding~\cite{nataraj2011malware}. Like
\texttt{recurrence\_plot}, this representation is 2-dimensional (height $\times$ width, 1 channel).
\item \textbf{rgb\_stack.} A 3-channel (red/green/blue) numeric-to-pixel image: red encodes packet
rate and the five TCP-flag fractions, green encodes the six protocol-mix fractions, and blue
encodes average/std packet size (zero-padded from two to six columns so all three channels share
one width); each channel is independently min-max-normalized before being stacked, the way a
standard color photograph combines three separate grayscale channels. No axis labels, legend, or
colorbar. Unlike the other five representations, this one is genuinely 3-dimensional (height
$\times$ width $\times$ 3 channels) rather than a 2D grid rendered through a colormap -- the direct
network-traffic analogue of CiNeT's bijective network-to-RGB-image encoding~\cite{bijective2025}.
\end{enumerate}

Exact rendering code is in \texttt{src/imgrep/visualization\_variants.py}, released with this paper.
Figure~\ref{fig:reps} shows all six representations rendered from one identical sampled CIC-IoT-2023
window (a benign traffic sample), generated by \texttt{scripts/generate\_figures.py} directly from
the real \texttt{viz/} output of the confirmatory run.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_representations_example.png}
\caption{The six image representations compared in this study, rendered from the identical
underlying traffic window (sample \texttt{Benign\_Final\_0000}). Left to right: full annotation
(\texttt{four\_panel}, \texttt{single\_line}, \texttt{heatmap}) through zero annotation
(\texttt{recurrence\_plot}, \texttt{grayscale\_matrix}, \texttt{rgb\_stack}).}
\label{fig:reps}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Fixed prompting condition}

Every call in this study uses a single, fixed prompting condition: RAG-grounded, restricting the
model's citation menu to the same 31-entry hand-verified CAPEC/CVE corpus used and released by the
companion study~\cite{groundingcitation2026}, reused here unchanged
(\texttt{src/imgrep/corpus.py}). We chose RAG-grounded specifically because the companion study
found it the condition with the best citation-correctness properties for both models tested; testing
representation effects against the strongest-known grounding technique, rather than the weakest, is
the more informative and more conservative test -- if representation still moves citation quality
substantially even under the best available grounding, that is the stronger and more actionable
finding. Because each representation needs its own short caption describing what the image shows
and how to read it (necessary for \texttt{recurrence\_plot}, \texttt{grayscale\_matrix}, and
\texttt{rgb\_stack}, which carry no in-image annotation at all), the only text that varies across
the six per-representation prompts is that one caption; the classification instructions, the full
serialized reference corpus, the citation restriction (``cite ONLY a reference ID from the database
above''), and the required response format are byte-identical across all six
(\texttt{src/imgrep/prompting.py}, unit-tested in \texttt{tests/test\_prompting.py}).

\subsection{Models and scale}

We run the identical (sample, representation) manifest against two models: Qwen-vl-plus (Alibaba,
via DashScope) and GPT-4o-mini (OpenAI), via \texttt{scripts/run\_pipeline\_qwen.py} and
\texttt{scripts/run\_pipeline\_gpt4o.py} respectively (a third client, for Claude Sonnet, is
released alongside these two but was not run for the results reported below -- see
Section~\ref{sec:limitations}). Both scripts share the same resumable-checkpoint pattern as the
companion study's harnesses: results are written incrementally to CSV, a rerun with the same
arguments skips already-completed (\texttt{sample\_id}, \texttt{representation}) pairs and retries
only \texttt{ERROR} rows, so an interrupted run never re-spends already-paid-for API calls.

The confirmatory run reported in Section~\ref{sec:results} used a 10-class subset of the CIC-IoT-2023
taxonomy (\texttt{Benign\_Final}, \texttt{DDoS-HTTP\_Flood}, \texttt{DDoS-SYN\_Flood},
\texttt{DDoS-SynonymousIP\_Flood}, \texttt{DNS\_Spoofing}, \texttt{DictionaryBruteForce},
\texttt{MITM-ArpSpoofing}, \texttt{Mirai-greip\_flood}, \texttt{Mirai-udpplain},
\texttt{SqlInjection}), 102 sampled windows per class ($n=1{,}020$ windows total, exactly balanced
across classes) $\times$ 6 representations $=$ 6,120 API calls per model, 12,240 calls total across
both models. Every call completed with zero \texttt{ERROR} rows for both models -- no representation
or class has a reduced $n$ from retried failures. Real, confirmed cost: \$20.00 for an earlier
Claude Sonnet 5 pilot at this same $n=1{,}020$ scale (used only to calibrate
\texttt{EMPIRICAL\_COST\_PER\_CALL}, not included in the results below), and, for the two models
actually reported, approximately \$15 (Qwen-vl-plus) and \$16 (GPT-4o-mini, confirmed real) for the
full 6,120-call run each.

\subsection{Grading and analysis}

Classification accuracy and citation quality are graded with the exact same rubric as the companion
study (\texttt{src/imgrep/grading.py}, reused with only cosmetic axis-label changes from
``by condition'' to ``by representation'' -- the underlying \texttt{wilson\_ci()} and
\texttt{grade\_citation()} logic is unchanged): every citation is graded
\texttt{real-and-correct}, \texttt{real-but-generic}, \texttt{real-but-wrong-family},
\texttt{no-citation}, \texttt{n/a-benign}, or \texttt{UNVERIFIED} against the same corpus, and every
proportion reported uses a Wilson 95\% confidence interval~\cite{wilson1927} rather than a normal
approximation. The primary comparison is real-and-correct citation rate among attack (non-benign)
samples, broken out by representation, with non-overlapping confidence intervals between
representations treated as the threshold for a representation effect large enough to matter
practically. Classification accuracy by representation is reported as a secondary outcome, mirroring
the companion study's finding that a grounding technique can leave citation quality and
classification accuracy to move independently.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

All results below are computed directly from the real, completed harness output
(\texttt{results\_1020/harness\_results\_gpt4o.csv} and
\texttt{results\_1020/harness\_results\_qwen.csv}, zero \texttt{ERROR} rows in either file) by
\texttt{scripts/generate\_figures.py}, using the same \texttt{wilson\_ci()} and
\texttt{grade\_citation()} logic as \texttt{summary.txt}, so the figures, tables, and prose below
cannot silently disagree with each other. $n=1{,}020$ per representation per model throughout;
$n=918$ (non-benign) for citation-rate denominators.

\subsection{Classification accuracy by representation}

Figure~\ref{fig:accuracy} and Table~\ref{tab:accuracy} show a sharp two-cluster split that holds for
both models. The three annotated representations (\texttt{four\_panel}, \texttt{single\_line},
\texttt{heatmap}) sustain 42--80\% accuracy; the three unannotated/dimensionality-varying
representations (\texttt{recurrence\_plot}, \texttt{grayscale\_matrix}, \texttt{rgb\_stack}) collapse
to 9.7--11.1\% for both models -- statistically indistinguishable from the 10\% base rate of a
balanced 10-class task. Confidence intervals do not overlap between the two clusters for either
model. Within the annotated cluster the two models diverge: GPT-4o-mini favors \texttt{four\_panel}
(71.9\%) over \texttt{single\_line} (41.8\%) and \texttt{heatmap} (44.7\%), while Qwen-vl-plus is
roughly flat across all three (77.5--80.4\%, \texttt{heatmap} numerically highest). Annotation
richness, not information content, appears to drive the accuracy cluster split: \texttt{single\_line}
carries only one of fourteen feature channels yet performs like the fully annotated \texttt{heatmap}
and \texttt{four\_panel} for both models, while \texttt{grayscale\_matrix} carries all fourteen
channels yet performs at chance -- isolating annotation, not channel count, as the operative variable.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Classification accuracy by representation, Wilson 95\% CI, $n=1{,}020$ per cell.}
\label{tab:accuracy}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Representation & GPT-4o-mini & Qwen-vl-plus \\
\midrule
four\_panel        & 71.9\% [69.0--74.5\%] & 77.5\% [74.9--80.0\%] \\
single\_line        & 41.8\% [38.8--44.8\%] & 79.4\% [76.8--81.8\%] \\
heatmap             & 44.7\% [41.7--47.8\%] & 80.4\% [77.8--82.7\%] \\
recurrence\_plot    & 9.8\% [8.1--11.8\%]   & 10.0\% [8.3--12.0\%]  \\
grayscale\_matrix   & 9.7\% [8.0--11.7\%]   & 10.0\% [8.3--12.0\%]  \\
rgb\_stack          & 11.1\% [9.3--13.2\%]  & 10.0\% [8.3--12.0\%]  \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_accuracy_by_representation.png}
\caption{Classification accuracy by representation and model, Wilson 95\% CI error bars. Dashed line
marks the 10-class chance rate.}
\label{fig:accuracy}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Citation quality by representation}

Citation quality (Figure~\ref{fig:citation}, Table~\ref{tab:citation}) tracks the same two-cluster
split as accuracy, but with a notable divergence: Qwen-vl-plus's citation quality on the annotated
cluster is meaningfully \emph{better} than GPT-4o-mini's, most sharply on \texttt{heatmap} (61.7\% vs.
31.4\%) and \texttt{single\_line} (55.8\% vs. 32.7\%), despite the two models' accuracy being closer
on those same representations. On the unannotated cluster, both models converge to
$\approx$11\%, again statistically at the level expected from citing the single most common attack
family by chance in a 10-class corpus. One \texttt{grayscale\_matrix} citation from GPT-4o-mini
returned an ID not in the verified corpus (\texttt{UNVERIFIED}, 3 of 1,020 rows) and was excluded
from the real-and-correct denominator pending manual verification against MITRE/NVD.

