\caption{Real-and-correct citation rate among attack samples, by representation and model, Wilson
95\% CI error bars.}
\label{fig:citation}
\end{figure}

\subsection{No-citation rate by representation, and Qwen-vl-plus's total collapse}
\label{sec:no-citation}

This is the sharpest finding in the study, and it runs directly counter to H2. If low annotation
pushed models toward conservative, hedged responses, the \texttt{no-citation} rate should rise on
the three unannotated representations. It does the opposite for both models. GPT-4o-mini's
\texttt{no-citation} rate is 24.7\% on \texttt{four\_panel} and 42.2\% on \texttt{single\_line}, but
falls to 2.1\% (\texttt{recurrence\_plot}), 16.2\% (\texttt{grayscale\_matrix}), and 0.5\%
(\texttt{rgb\_stack}) -- the model volunteers \emph{more}, not fewer, citations when it can least
support them, and 651--816 of every 1,020 unannotated-representation rows land in
\texttt{real-but-wrong-family} rather than \texttt{no-citation}.

Qwen-vl-plus shows the same pattern in its most extreme possible form. Its \texttt{no-citation} rate
on the unannotated cluster is exactly \textbf{0.0\%} across all three representations (0 of 1,020
rows each) -- and inspecting the raw predictions shows why: on \texttt{recurrence\_plot},
\texttt{grayscale\_matrix}, and \texttt{rgb\_stack} alike, Qwen-vl-plus predicts the identical class
(\texttt{DDoS-SYN\_Flood}) and cites the identical reference (\texttt{CAPEC-482}) on every single one
of the 3,060 calls, independent of the true class or the actual image content. The
citation-grade breakdown is identical across all three representations: 816 \texttt{real-but-wrong-family}
(the 816 non-\texttt{DDoS-SYN\_Flood}, non-benign samples), 102 \texttt{n/a-benign} (the benign
samples, correctly excluded from grading), and 102 \texttt{real-and-correct} -- which is not evidence
of 102 correct classifications so much as the model happening to be right exactly when the true class
is \texttt{DDoS-SYN\_Flood} (102 of 1,020 samples, matching the class's balanced share). This is total
output collapse, not degraded-but-varied performance: the model produces zero visual information from
these three representations and instead emits one fixed, maximally confident answer regardless of
input.

Both findings support the same conclusion: under low-annotation representations, both models fail by
confident fabrication, not by honest hedging. This is direct evidence for a fabrication-under-uncertainty
mechanism (a variant of H1) over the elaboration/over-hedging mechanism (H2) -- stripping visual
annotation does not make either model more conservative, it makes both models guess with undiminished,
and in Qwen-vl-plus's case totally undifferentiated, confidence.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

The results do not support a monotonic annotation gradient; they support a binary threshold. Both
models treat \texttt{four\_panel}, \texttt{single\_line}, and \texttt{heatmap} as members of one
functional cluster and \texttt{recurrence\_plot}, \texttt{grayscale\_matrix}, and \texttt{rgb\_stack}
as members of a second, regardless of how much information or how many channels each representation
actually carries -- \texttt{single\_line}'s single channel performs like \texttt{heatmap}'s fourteen,
and \texttt{grayscale\_matrix}'s fourteen channels perform like nothing at all. This is the cleanest
possible separation of an annotation effect from an information effect that the study's design set
out to find: annotation, not channel count, is what determines whether the model can use an image at
all. \texttt{rgb\_stack} -- the representation that varies dimensionality rather than annotation
richness -- patterns entirely with the other two unannotated representations rather than forming a
distinct third cluster, for both models. That is itself informative: a genuinely 3-dimensional
(height $\times$ width $\times$ channel) encoding earns the model no more purchase than a 2D grayscale
grid once textual annotation is removed, suggesting dimensionality is not the operative variable
either -- annotation is.

The two models diverge sharply within the annotated cluster (GPT-4o-mini favors \texttt{four\_panel}
specifically; Qwen-vl-plus performs comparably well on all three annotated representations, and
matches or beats GPT-4o-mini's citation quality on every one of them), which mirrors the companion
study's finding that a technique's effect can be model-dependent even when the underlying
qualitative pattern (here, the annotated/unannotated split) replicates across models. But the
Section~\ref{sec:no-citation} finding -- that neither model's \texttt{no-citation} rate rises on the
unannotated cluster, and that Qwen-vl-plus's failure mode there is a single, fixed, maximally
confident wrong answer repeated across all 3,060 calls -- is the study's most actionable result, and
it replicates in the same direction for both models despite their otherwise different behavior. This
directly rejects H2 as the operative mechanism in this setting: an elaboration-driven hallucination
story predicts hedging under uncertainty, and neither model hedges. It instead supports a
fabrication-under-uncertainty account (a variant of H1): when a multimodal LLM cannot visually parse
an image, it does not become more cautious, it becomes uniformly, confidently wrong -- the specific
failure mode a citation-grounded security pipeline most needs to detect, because it is
indistinguishable from a correct, confident answer without independently verifying the citation
against the corpus, exactly the check this study's grading rubric performs and a naive
accuracy-only evaluation would miss entirely.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

This paper's central limitation, stated plainly, is scope on two axes. First, models: the
confirmatory results in Section~\ref{sec:results} cover GPT-4o-mini and Qwen-vl-plus only. A Claude
Sonnet 5 client (\texttt{src/imgrep/claude\_client.py}) is released alongside these two and was
pilot-tested at this study's $n=1{,}020$ scale (real, confirmed cost \$20.00) to calibrate cost
estimates, but a full confirmatory Claude Sonnet run against this specific 10-class manifest was not
completed for this paper -- Qwen-vl-plus was substituted as the second model primarily on cost
grounds (approximately \$15 vs. \$120 for a full 6,120-call run, real observed rates for both). The
qualitative pattern reported here (annotated/unannotated cluster split, no-citation rate falling
rather than rising under low annotation) should be treated as established for two models, not three,
until a Claude Sonnet leg is added. Second, classes: the confirmatory run used a 10-class subset of
CIC-IoT-2023 (Section~\ref{sec:methods}) rather than the full taxonomy; extending to the full class
set is future work and could reveal representation effects specific to attack families not sampled
here. Beyond that: the six representations were chosen to span an annotation
gradient plus one dimensionality axis using established, precedented encodings
(Sections~\ref{sec:representations} and~\ref{sec:related}), but they are not an exhaustive space of
possible traffic-image encodings, and a null or small effect across these six would not rule out a
larger effect from a representation not tested here. The recurrence-plot, grayscale-matrix, and
rgb-stack representations' per-feature normalization is computed per-window rather than against a
corpus-wide reference distribution, so extreme windows may normalize differently than a deployed
system using a fixed normalization would; this matches the companion study's per-window rendering
convention but is worth flagging as a modeling choice. \texttt{rgb\_stack}'s blue channel is
additionally zero-padded (two real feature columns padded to six to match the other channels'
width), which introduces a fixed, uninformative region of every image that is not present in any
other representation -- worth checking as a possible confound if \texttt{rgb\_stack} behaves
differently from the rest. Finally, as in the companion study, GPT-4o-mini API calls may fail transiently
(rate limits, timeouts); such failures are logged as \texttt{ERROR} rows and automatically retried on
rerun via the resumable-checkpoint design, but a final reported $n$ per representation may be
slightly uneven across representations if some fail more persistently than others, and this should
be reported explicitly alongside the accuracy/citation tables in Section~\ref{sec:results} rather
than silently excluded.

\section{Conclusion}

Citation-grounded multimodal LLM classification of network traffic has been shown to reduce
citation hallucination through prompting-level grounding, but whether that benefit is robust to how
the underlying traffic is visually encoded has not been studied. This paper isolates image
representation as an independent variable -- holding prompting condition, dataset, models, citation
corpus, and grading rubric fixed -- across a deliberate five-way annotation gradient from a fully
labeled composite chart to a raw, unannotated numeric-to-pixel image, plus a sixth, genuinely
3-dimensional RGB-stack encoding, and releases working, resumable, dual-model experiment code so the
question can be answered empirically. Against a real, completed $n=1{,}020$ run on CIC-IoT-2023, the
answer is that image representation matters enormously, but through a binary annotated/unannotated
threshold rather than a smooth gradient, and the failure mode under the unannotated threshold is
confident fabrication rather than honest hedging for both models tested -- a representation choice
that looks cosmetic can silently convert a citation-grounded pipeline's output from mostly correct to
uniformly, confidently wrong.

